# Šank behind Caddy — the whole of it.
#
# `deploy/nginx.conf` does the same job and is the longer road: a 170-line
# config, certbot installed separately, a renewal timer to trust. Caddy fetches
# the certificate the first time somebody loads the page and renews it on its
# own, and this file is three lines. Either is fine; pick one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Install it (on the server)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   sudo apt-get install -y caddy
#   sudo nano /etc/caddy/Caddyfile      # paste the block below
#   sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# Point the hostname's A record at this server first and leave 80 and 443 open
# — `setup-server.sh` already opens them. Caddy proves it owns the name over
# port 80, so a firewall that closes it is the usual reason a certificate never
# arrives.
#
# A **DuckDNS** subdomain works exactly like any other name here: it is a real
# domain as far as Let's Encrypt is concerned, and the ordinary HTTP challenge
# below needs no API token (that is only for wildcards). Set the subdomain's IP
# once in the DuckDNS panel.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sharing a server with another app
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One Caddyfile holds as many of these blocks as there are hostnames. Šank
# listens on **3100** and nothing else on the box may: `NITRO_PORT` in
# /opt/sank/.env, `sank.service` and `HEALTH_URL` in deploy/.deploy.env all
# name that port, and they have to keep agreeing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The block
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

sank.example.duckdns.org {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3100
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why there is nothing else in it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **The forwarded address.** `TRUST_PROXY=1` in /opt/sank/.env tells the app to
# believe the forwarded headers, and it must only ever be true behind a proxy
# that writes them itself — a header is otherwise whatever the caller typed,
# and every PIN lockout and rate-limit bucket would be chosen by the attacker.
# `clientIp()` (server/utils/auth.ts) reads `X-Real-IP` first and otherwise the
# **last** hop of `X-Forwarded-For`. Caddy's `reverse_proxy` appends the address
# it is actually talking to, so the last hop is the truth and a spoofed header
# only adds hops in front of it. Nothing to configure — but do not add a
# `header_up` that rewrites either header, and do not put another proxy in
# front of Caddy without reading that function first.
#
# **Uploads.** Delivery photos post to `/api/uploads`; nginx needs
# `client_max_body_size 10m` for them and Caddy has no such limit by default,
# so there is nothing to raise.
#
# **The app is not on the internet by itself.** It binds 127.0.0.1:3100 and the
# firewall never opens that port. Caddy is the only way in, which is what makes
# `SANK_DEV_ENROL` absent from the production .env a real protection rather
# than a hopeful one.
#
# **https is not optional here.** The session and device cookies are `Secure`
# unless `COOKIE_SECURE=0` (a dev laptop setting, never the server), and the
# waiter app is an installable PWA whose service worker browsers will not
# register over plain http. There is no "try it on the bare IP first" step.
